{
  "version": 268,
  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v268: EVENT IDENTITY, PHASE I2 — THE BINDER. I0 settled what a record means and I1 taught the fold to APPLY one. This is the first phase that DECIDES one. The controlling design is lifehug-platform `docs/design/event-identity.md` v4 (§4.1 retrieval and the plausibility filter, §4.2 rung R1, §4.5 the safeguards, §5.6 the re-audit, §6.1's caps and §8's dry run); this repo's summary is ADR 0031. Nothing about the fold moves, `CALCULATION_RULE_VERSION` stays `timeline-rules:5`, and `compile` stays zero-model — the binder runs where records are MADE, as a maintenance step and as the `bind-episodes` verb, and never inside a compile.\n\nNOTHING IS WRITTEN UNLESS SOMEBODY TYPED `--apply`. The verb is dry by default, `--dry-run` WINS when both flags are typed, and the weekly maintenance step is dry BY CONSTRUCTION — rollout step 3 says no live bind before I3 ships confirmation, split and possible_overmerge, and a scheduled writer would be one. What the scheduled step produces is the report the owner reviews once: which tellings look like one event, EVERY condition each pair passed and failed, and how many \"when did this happen?\" questions an apply would end.\n\nRETRIEVE, THEN DECIDE. Candidates share any of §4.1's six blocking signals — a resolved non-owner participant, a normalized place, an era membership, overlapping outer bounds inside ONE age frame (`cross_dating.frame_for`, the one definition), a proper-noun label token of four characters or more, the same source document — and a zero-model score of one point per independent signal gates everything downstream. Below the floor a candidate is dropped SILENTLY: absence is not a decision, and a queue full of \"these two things share the word Tuesday\" is a question surface nobody reads. Wide retrieval is cheap; questions are not.\n\nR1 IS §4.2'S SEVEN CONDITIONS, IN §4.2'S ORDER, WITH §4.2'S NAMES. A kind-family table in code, with `moment` a wildcard and not a solvent — `job` and `school` still share no family, and `idea` shares none with anything, which is why the founder's own `the idea for Etherfuse · 2021` is refused by the kind-family gate and becomes a question. Repeatable protection: an undated telling is NEVER auto-bound to a `job`-class episode, because a missing discriminator is exactly how a second stint at one employer silently becomes an edit of the first. Exact label stems over a fixed twenty-one-entry event-verb table, so \"Started Etherfuse\" and \"Co-founded Etherfuse with AJ\" both reduce to `etherfuse-found` — and an unrecognized verb stays a subject token, which can only ever make a stem more specific and refuse a bind. Two independent signals beyond the owner AND beyond the label. Exactly one surviving candidate. No active or entailed `not_same`. And never joining two episodes that each hold two or more tellings. A label-only match is a PROPOSAL and binds nothing; two survivors are one work item naming both.\n\nTHE SAFEGUARDS SHIP WITH R1, NOT AFTER IT. Members of one episode whose STATED outer bounds are disjoint mint one `possible_overmerge` item and never an automatic split — an automatic split is this design's own defect mirrored. The bridge diagnostic is computed over the OPERATION GRAPH and names the single receipt holding two halves of an episode together; an episode joined by exactly one receipt is reported as a sole receipt rather than as a bridge, because a diagnostic that fires on every episode is a diagnostic nobody reads. Time-decay: a wide-gap place mismatch is part_of-suggestive, never a veto. And every one of §5.6's nine re-audit triggers runs through C4's `reaudit`, whose only outcomes are mint-one-item and do-nothing — a re-audit structurally cannot move, split, keep or re-confirm a bind.\n\nQUESTION OUTPUTS, NOT QUESTIONS. Pairs are emitted as DATA: C4's pair key, the candidate's members, every failed condition, and the inputs the EXISTING work-item value scoring reads — never a score of its own, because §4.1 says identity pairs are eligible by value and their kind never outranks keystones by fiat. §6.1's caps mark at most one pair per telling and at most twenty-five overall as surfaced, and NOTHING is dropped: dropping a pair below the cap would make the cap a silent decision about which of a person's questions exist. Neither `same_event` nor `possible_overmerge` is registered in `WORK_ITEM_KINDS`, and a test asserts the absence: I3 owns the probe, the five answers and the filing, and a kind whose answer nothing can file is the silent under-delivery ADR 0021 refuses.\n\nTHE WRITES GO THROUGH I0'S OWN WRITERS. An accepted pair becomes one `create` envelope filed by `file_operation_envelope` — bindings first, operation last — and the operation id digests the MEMBER SET and nothing else: no invocation id, no wall clock, no `acted_on_episode_ids`. So deleting every deterministic identity record and re-running on the same durable inputs reproduces the same operation ids, episode ids and bindings byte-for-byte, which is G1's release promise checked at the binder rather than asserted about it. A pair is nameable before it exists, too: a prospective candidate is keyed by the episode id a `yes` would create, so the question and the episode that answers it are one id.\n\nTHREE PLACES THE DESIGN HAD TO BE READ RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBED, each named in a constant rather than found in a diff. `CLUSTER_RULE_TEXT`: §4.2 names no envelope for a telling joining an episode that already exists and C2's operation vocabulary has no `add`, so the deterministic act is ONE `create` over the whole cluster and growth reuses §3.2's own supersede-with-alias — while an ADOPTED episode, or one made by human authority, is never moved by a rule and gets a proposal instead. `NON_TRANSITIVE_RULE_TEXT`: Law 3 — a bind is accepted only when both sides chose each other and no third telling chose the same unit in the same run, because a group formed by transitivity is a membership nobody decided. And the containment record lands as `proposed` rather than `deterministic`, because C2's validator pins the narrow reading that a deterministic origin binds `same` and nothing else — a `proposed` record changes no drawing, renders as the node's `proposed_links` and ranks the question.\n\nPROVEN TO FIRE. Twenty-five deliberate one-line mutations were each applied to a built tree and the suite SEEN going red: the plausibility floor at zero; every kind compatible; repeatable protection always passing; label stems always matching; one independent signal enough; two survivors accepted; a `not_same` that never blocks; two mature episodes joining; containment on any shared token instead of the whole name; a prospective unit as a container; two containers treated as a pick; a one-sided bind; a clock in the operation digest; every pair surfaced; the over-merge audit silenced; a sole receipt called a bridge; time-decay never firing; an adopted episode moved by a rule; the maintenance step writing; the containment record claiming a deterministic origin; the label stem keeping the cast; a telling about an era treated as a unit; `--apply` beating `--dry-run`; the weekly step applying; and the caps dropping what they do not surface. Four of the twenty-five were GREEN on the first sweep and each named a real hole in the tests rather than in the code — the guards for the label rung, the whole-name containment rule, the per-telling surfacing cap and the adopted-episode protection were written, then seen failing, then kept.",
